00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.260
Thank you.

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:50.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'd like to
begin with a question about one

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.680
of the most mysterious and
important phases of life, which

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.480
is puberty.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:02.130
I've long wondered whether or
not how quickly somebody goes

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.200
into puberty-- so at what age--

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.900
and how long puberty
takes-- so how

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.520
brief or protracted
that puberty is

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.220
for them to acquire the
so-called secondary sexual

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.290
characteristics,
things like hair growth

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:17.190
on the face for males and
changes in bone, and muscle

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.230
density, and growth, et cetera.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.672
When I was in middle
school and high school,

00:08:21.672 --> 00:08:23.880
I noticed that some people
transitioned into all that

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:27.720
very fast and some people
took a long time to acquire

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.330
those characteristics.

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:34.000
Can we learn anything about
ourselves, our hormones,

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.770
and maybe even how
long we're going

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:39.280
to live based on the time
in which we enter puberty

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:42.090
and how long it takes us to
progress through puberty?

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:43.840
And I guess that also
raises the question,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.660
does puberty ever truly end?

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:49.030
KYLE GILLETT: There are
many takeaways from puberty.

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:54.190
Some of the actionable items
from it are, yes, it can

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:58.090
and does affect your adult
height and also stature

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:00.740
and also body composition.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:02.935
So puberty is a time--

00:09:02.935 --> 00:09:07.150
and if we're talking
specifically about males,

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:13.000
think of it as a time where,
if you have obesity as a child,

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.910
you could potentially
use that time

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:18.610
to change your lifestyle
and habits and reset things,

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:20.570
and it is a bit easier.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:23.560
It's almost like
a free injection

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.650
of testosterone and metabolism
and drive and effort

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:27.940
into your life.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:33.260
There is a wide variation in how
quickly puberty goes through.

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:36.130
So there are stages called
Tanner stages, which we don't

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:37.700
necessarily need to get into.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:40.180
But if you enter
puberty very early,

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:45.010
then it can decrease your
adult height or stature.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:48.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So for a
given male that enters puberty

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:52.300
at 13 versus a male that
enters puberty at 15,

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.840
can we say that the guy
that entered puberty at 13

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.530
is going to be shorter than the
guy that entered puberty at 15,

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:01.420
or it's not quite
that straightforward?

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:03.670
KYLE GILLETT: If they
are identical twins

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:05.440
and the individual
who entered puberty

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:09.010
at age 13 also finished
puberty, went all the way

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:10.160
through the Tanner stages--

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:11.910
and if you do a bone
scan, which I believe

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:13.930
is usually done
on the left wrist,

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:16.450
and it says yes, your growth
plates are mostly closed,

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:19.180
you're not going to grow more
than a couple inches of height

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.550
after that.

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:23.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Just
a related question.

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:26.440
When I was growing
up, it was thought,

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.800
or at least people would say,
that resistance training--

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:31.840
in particular,
lifting heavy weights

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.520
could stunt one's growth.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.920
Is that true or false?

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:38.780
KYLE GILLETT: It is
false when you're

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:40.640
talking about just
lifting heavy weights.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.640
Dirty bulking certainly
has the potential to stunt

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:45.510
one's growth for
two main mechanisms.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Could
you define dirty bulking?

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:49.520
KYLE GILLETT: [CHUCKLES]
So dirty bulking

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:54.110
is eating an excess of
calories not just to acquire

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:57.890
lean metabolically active
body mass or get stronger,

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:02.580
but purposely
acquiring body fat.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:04.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
purposely acquiring muscle

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:09.080
and fat by overeating
and lifting weights

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:10.670
can stunt one's growth.

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:11.780
Do I have that correct?

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:12.740
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:14.510
So it does two things.

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:17.450
If you're doing it as a
very young child, it can--

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:20.540
that fat can become
leptin resistant

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:23.840
and it can produce more
leptin, and that leptin

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.060
can activate the
hypothalamus, which

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:29.420
activates the pituitary, which
releases gonadotropin, which

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.190
basically just
increase testosterone

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.620
and estrogen earlier than
it otherwise would have.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:37.670
It's the same mechanism behind
why childhood obesity causes

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:38.562
early puberty.

00:11:38.562 --> 00:11:39.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:42.740
I do remember a paper
published in Science Magazine--

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:44.990
I believe it was focused
mainly on females,

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:48.003
but showing that when
enough body fat accumulates,

00:11:48.003 --> 00:11:49.670
the hormone leptin
is secreted, and that

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:50.973
triggers the onset of puberty.

00:11:50.973 --> 00:11:51.890
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:55.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Given the
increase in childhood obesity

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:58.070
that we're observing
now, are we seeing

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:01.760
an earlier onset of puberty
in males and females?

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:03.998
KYLE GILLETT: Yes, in
both males and females.

00:12:03.998 --> 00:12:05.540
Not to get too
technical, but there's

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:08.180
a G protein-coupled
receptor on the hypothalamus

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:10.340
and leptin directly
binds it, so it

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:13.520
does appear directly
cause causatory and not

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:14.482
just correlation.

00:12:14.482 --> 00:12:16.440
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And if
I understand correctly,

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:19.880
what you're saying is
for a young guy-- let's

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.460
say, 13, 14-- who
wants to really bulk up

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.933
and deliberately-- deliberately,
excuse me-- overeats, and is

00:12:25.933 --> 00:12:28.100
doing their squats, and
deadlifts, and bench presses

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:32.570
and really trying to get big,
they will get big, but only

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:34.010
in the lateral dimension.

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:37.940
They're effectively
limiting their total height

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:41.570
and it can shut down the long
bone growth of their limbs.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:42.470
Is that correct?

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:43.387
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

00:12:43.387 --> 00:12:45.800
The growth of the long
bones is mostly related

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:48.530
to the estradiol alpha
receptor, so basically,

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.520
one of the receptors
for estrogen,

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:54.530
which can be secondary
to early puberty and also

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:57.110
is related to body fat because
you have that conversion

00:12:57.110 --> 00:13:00.160
of testosterone to estrogen.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:04.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So can we
assume that if a young male

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:08.280
wants to get into resistance
training that bodyweight

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:11.820
exercises are probably OK
and maybe even some weight

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:13.560
training, kettlebells,
et cetera,

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:16.800
but that they should avoid
doing so-called dirty bulking--

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.170
trying to deliberately
gain weight--

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:20.490
up until what age?

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:23.070
Until puberty is over?

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:25.470
KYLE GILLETT: I would say
an individual should limit

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:29.550
the amount of abnormal
body fat accumulation,

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:32.652
or dirty bulking, indefinitely
throughout their entire life.

00:13:32.652 --> 00:13:34.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So again,
if I understand correctly,

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:38.250
that recommendation to
avoid deliberate weight gain

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:43.020
or rapid weight gain is not
just to allow an individual

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:47.520
to reach their maximum height,
but also to avoid laying down

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:48.780
a lot of body fat cells.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:49.663
Correct?

00:13:49.663 --> 00:13:50.580
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:52.710
The balance between
that is, when

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.510
you are going
through puberty, you

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:56.940
are able to add a lot
of lean body mass--

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:00.505
not just muscle mass, but bone
mass and other mass as well.

00:14:00.505 --> 00:14:02.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I started
lifting weights when I was 16,

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:06.702
and I confess I trained
pretty heavy at times.

00:14:06.702 --> 00:14:08.160
I don't know whether
or not I would

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:11.310
have been taller than
I am now, but when

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:13.740
I started that
training, I had already

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:17.940
reached what was at least
close to my predicted height.

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:20.497
I can't say that I deliberately
waited until I had grown.

00:14:20.497 --> 00:14:22.830
It just so happened that I
stumbled into the weight room

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:26.170
and found that I liked it
at age 16, at which point,

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:28.200
I was already the
height that I am now.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:30.960
So in any case,
what I'm hearing is

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:34.500
that laying down a lot of excess
body fat is not a good idea.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:39.828
What if somebody grows up chubby
or fat for whatever reason--

00:14:39.828 --> 00:14:42.120
reasons related to the eating
patterns in their family,

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:43.770
maybe even some genetic reasons.

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:48.120
Is it safe and/or wise
for a young person--

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.090
so let's say somebody who's
around the age of puberty,

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:54.780
or even younger, or in their
late teens-- to be dieting

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:56.460
and actively trying
to lose body fat.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:58.105
Is that safe?

00:14:58.105 --> 00:15:00.230
KYLE GILLETT: Under the
supervision of a physician,

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:03.140
it is certainly safe to
change your body composition.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:05.780
In pediatric obesity
medicine, you're

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:08.420
often talking about
a recomposition

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:12.842
or a renormalization of the
growth curve compared to peers.

00:15:12.842 --> 00:15:13.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:14.450
Thank you.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:17.540
So as you may have sensed,
we started chronologically

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.250
with puberty, and
I know that there's

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:22.130
another puberty that even
precedes the puberty that we're

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:23.248
all familiar with.

00:15:23.248 --> 00:15:25.040
Maybe-- if you want to
just briefly mention

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:26.957
that because I was talking
with you about this

00:15:26.957 --> 00:15:27.830
before we started.

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:30.462
The puberty that I'm
most familiar with,

00:15:30.462 --> 00:15:32.420
and I think most people
are most familiar with,

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:35.000
the acquisition of
deepening of the voice,

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:39.465
growth of muscle and bone, body
hair, acquisition of libido

00:15:39.465 --> 00:15:40.340
and things like that.

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:42.923
That's actually the second
puberty that we all go through.

00:15:42.923 --> 00:15:44.840
Maybe would just mention
for us and educate us

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:45.950
on the first puberty.

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:49.190
I think most people
will be hearing

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:50.870
this for the very first time.

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:53.150
KYLE GILLETT: The first
puberty of everyone's life

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:55.190
is the first three
months of their life.

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:59.180
You may notice that your baby
has more acne the first three

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:04.310
months and that they also have,
in general, just more changes

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:09.500
related to androgens and
estrogens, perhaps oilier skin,

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.570
even more genitourinary--

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:14.430
genital growth during
the first three months.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:18.620
And this is mostly due to DHEA,
which is an adrenal hormone.

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:20.108
The second puberty,
or the puberty

00:16:20.108 --> 00:16:22.400
that most people know of,
actually starts that same way

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:23.310
as well.

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:27.470
It's called adrenarche, and
it's when the adrenals kick in,

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:28.970
I guess, for the second time.

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:31.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is there
a standard age or age

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:35.300
range in which the
testicles descend in males?

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:37.010
KYLE GILLETT:
Usually before birth.

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:41.570
It is not uncommon to have one
or even two undescended testes,

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:43.710
but there is a risk
of testicular cancer,

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:46.490
especially if they are
not fixed early and also

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.687
heat damage to the teste.

00:16:48.687 --> 00:16:50.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
thank you for that coverage

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:51.910
of the two puberties.

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:52.810
So early in life.

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:55.450
I imagine some of our
listeners probably

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:58.055
still in one or the-- well,
one or the other puberty.

00:16:58.055 --> 00:16:59.680
The ones that are in
the first puberty,

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.060
obviously, aren't
aware that they're

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:02.860
listening to this
podcast, but maybe it'll

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:04.599
be embedded in
their subconscious.

00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:07.276
But some listeners probably
are still in puberty.

00:17:07.276 --> 00:17:09.609
But I think everyone can
remember back to their puberty,

00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:11.530
and roughly when they
first entered puberty,

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:14.560
and how quickly they
aggregated the secondary sex

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:15.940
characteristics.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:19.690
I'd like to turn now to a
general question about what

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:23.963
all males ought to do in order
to optimize their hormones.

00:17:23.963 --> 00:17:25.630
So if you could just
list off the things

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:29.590
that all males should do on
a daily basis, weekly basis.

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:31.750
I mean, should guys
in their teens and 20s

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.090
be getting their bloodwork
done, should they

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:35.153
be taking supplements?

00:17:35.153 --> 00:17:36.820
We already talked
about weight training.

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:38.737
What should they be doing
and what should they

00:17:38.737 --> 00:17:40.630
avoid doing if the
goal is to have

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:43.420
a long arc of healthy
hormone optimization

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:45.140
throughout the lifespan?

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:47.720
KYLE GILLETT: There's many
things that you should do.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:50.240
An analogy that I
often make is when

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:52.940
there's a brand new car that
comes off the assembly line,

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:55.310
you do a full scope
of diagnostic workup--

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:56.660
hook it up to the computer.

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:59.450
And I think we should do the
same thing with humans as well.

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:03.050
During puberty, obviously,
you're a functioning human,

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:06.240
but I would say there's
still development.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.810
And I think that the
human always develops.

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:10.520
I don't think
development ever ends,

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:12.590
but you want to
monitor that progress

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:16.780
across a person's lifespan.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:17.740
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:18.865
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, sorry.

00:18:18.865 --> 00:18:22.180
So for bloodwork-- I mean,
what would be the earliest--

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:23.320
let me put it this way.

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:27.970
If blood work didn't cost
anything and everyone

00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:29.590
could get it, when
would you want

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:31.480
to see everybody get
their bloodwork done

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:33.670
for the first time?

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:36.250
Obviously, individuals
under the age of 18

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.570
should talk with their
parents about this.

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:42.340
And as long as the
parents and the child

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:45.040
agree and the parents are
on board with this as well,

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.440
you can start getting bloodwork.

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:49.930
Often, a child will
come in with complaints

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.810
of either precocious
puberty or delayed puberty,

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:57.550
and this individual might be 9
or this individual might be 15.

00:18:57.550 --> 00:19:00.190
For a healthy
child, when they're

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:02.530
going through their later
Tanner stages-- which

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:05.020
is 4 and 5, so they've developed
several secondary sexual

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:08.980
characteristics-- they might
have hair growth or starting

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:11.740
to notice more beard growth.

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:14.000
That's a good time to do it.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:16.630
If you're concerned
with stature or heighth,

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:18.940
or if you're not
tracking along where most

00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:21.190
members of your family have--

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:24.010
not just their height
and stature, but also

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:28.660
the timing of their puberty,
then that's time to get labs.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So if I
could travel back in time,

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:34.540
I would have gotten my bloodwork
done for hormones, and lipids,

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:36.460
and everything else at 18.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:39.340
I, unfortunately, didn't know
where and how to get that,

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:41.480
and I didn't have any
pressing clinical issues.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:44.200
And so I think the first time
that I got my bloodwork done,

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:47.740
I was in my late 20s,
maybe even in my early 30s.

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:51.280
And I'm still dying to
know what my bloodwork was

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:54.400
when, for instance, I was
17 and I felt a certain way.

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:57.820
And I confess that
in many dimensions,

00:19:57.820 --> 00:19:59.740
I actually feel better now at--

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:03.460
I'll be 47 soon-- at 47 than
I did in my teens and 20s.

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:06.160
And I think it was more
on the psychological side.

00:20:06.160 --> 00:20:10.210
I think that-- but in
terms of just understanding

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:15.310
why we felt great or why we
felt or feel terrible or not so

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:17.830
great, I think bloodwork
is extremely informative.

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:19.780
What do you think
are the key things

00:20:19.780 --> 00:20:21.160
to look for in bloodwork?

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:23.740
I mean, testosterone is
always the topic that comes up

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:25.600
in the context of male
hormone optimisation,

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:27.558
but certainly there are
a lot of other hormones

00:20:27.558 --> 00:20:28.760
that are important as well.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:30.427
KYLE GILLETT: And
with testosterone, you

00:20:30.427 --> 00:20:32.470
want to get either
testosterone and an SHBG

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:33.695
or a free testosterone.

00:20:33.695 --> 00:20:36.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Could you
define SHBG for our listeners,

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:36.370
please?

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:38.412
KYLE GILLETT: It is Sex
Hormone Binding Globulin.

00:20:38.412 --> 00:20:41.680
It is the protein that
binds up all androgens

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:43.220
and estrogen in the body.

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:46.390
So the stronger the androgen,
the stronger it binds.

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:49.420
During puberty,
strong androgens,

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:51.670
especially DHT which
is the strongest

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:55.810
bioidentical androgen,
has a huge role--

00:20:55.810 --> 00:20:59.140
a prominent role in secondary
sexual characteristics.

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:02.620
And if your SHBG is
very high, then your DHT

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:05.080
can run higher because
it's not metabolized,

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:07.480
but there's not quite
as much free DHT.

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:12.250
So you want to balance
between a high enough free DHT

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:13.568
and a high enough total DHT.

00:21:13.568 --> 00:21:15.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
obviously, these blood tests

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:17.620
are going to have to
be read and interpreted

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.150
by a qualified physician.

00:21:19.150 --> 00:21:21.768
Most people aren't going to be
in a position to evaluate them

00:21:21.768 --> 00:21:24.310
properly, or at least not with
the full depth that they could

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:27.640
if they had an M.D like
yourself looking at them.

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:31.060
So everyone should get
bloodwork as early as possible

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:33.940
depending on their
budget and availability.

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:37.150
What should
everybody do in terms

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:39.040
of monitoring those markers?

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:41.620
So assuming that there's
no major intervention,

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:43.570
how often do you
recommend that people

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:46.138
get their bloodwork done?

00:21:46.138 --> 00:21:47.180
KYLE GILLETT: Let's say--

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:49.070
let's take an individual
who just turned 18,

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:50.780
they just got their
first set of bloodwork.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:52.322
They'll probably
find something in it

00:21:52.322 --> 00:21:55.520
that they may want to optimize
using shared decision-making

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:57.140
with their physician.

00:21:57.140 --> 00:22:00.020
Usually, a good follow-up
is about six months.

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:02.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So twice a
year, getting bloodwork done,

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:04.762
and then having a
physician evaluate it.

00:22:04.762 --> 00:22:05.970
That sounds reasonable to me.

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:08.510
And for those that didn't
initiate this at 18,

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:11.360
such as myself, I guess
the best time to start then

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:12.920
would be as soon as possible.

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:14.345
KYLE GILLETT: Yeah.

00:22:14.345 --> 00:22:16.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In
terms of the other things

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:20.480
that all males should do,
meaning all males of all ages--

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:25.140
puberty and beyond-- what
are some of those things?

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:27.530
So on a daily basis,
maybe you could just

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:30.830
take us through the arc
of a day and push out

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:32.420
some of the protocols
that you use

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:34.628
or the things that you like
to see your male patients

00:22:34.628 --> 00:22:37.365
use in order to try and
optimize their hormone status.

00:22:37.365 --> 00:22:39.740
KYLE GILLETT: I'll briefly
touch on some of the lifestyle

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:40.760
pillars to start.

00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:43.580
Diet and exercise
are the first to.

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:45.890
In puberty, sleep is
particularly important,

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:47.690
of course.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:52.970
But with diet and exercise,
throughout a lifespan,

00:22:52.970 --> 00:22:56.930
you want to not exclude
things that are helping you.

00:22:56.930 --> 00:22:59.090
For example, during
puberty if you're

00:22:59.090 --> 00:23:01.910
consuming dairy, and then all of
a sudden you cut out all dairy,

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:05.005
dairy can help increase
IGF-1 and free IGF-1.

00:23:05.005 --> 00:23:07.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And just,
again, for our audience,

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:10.400
maybe just mention what
having enough IGF-1 can

00:23:10.400 --> 00:23:12.047
do for us that's beneficial is?

00:23:12.047 --> 00:23:13.380
KYLE GILLETT: It helps you grow.

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:16.010
It helps with
genital development,

00:23:16.010 --> 00:23:19.430
secondary sexual
characteristics, and long bone

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:20.480
growth--

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:22.890
skin growth, hair
growth, a host of things.

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:25.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So getting
an array of nutrients

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:26.455
that include dairy.

00:23:26.455 --> 00:23:27.830
What other sorts
of nutrients are

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:30.363
important during development?

00:23:30.363 --> 00:23:32.780
KYLE GILLETT: You want to have
adequate vitamin D. Vitamin

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:34.490
D helps with
testosterone production,

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:39.590
it helps, again, with bone
mineralization and stature.

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:42.230
After an age of about 25--

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:46.040
and there's not a strict cutoff,
but up to about an age of 25,

00:23:46.040 --> 00:23:48.230
optimizing your growth
hormone and IGF-1

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:51.900
helps with bone density
and bone growth.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:54.230
So from the dietary
standpoint, you

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:57.170
want to have enough free
estrogen-- not too much

00:23:57.170 --> 00:23:58.010
when you're growing.

00:23:58.010 --> 00:24:02.630
But you want to help
basically stockpile bone

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:05.720
to prevent a risk of
osteoporosis or thin bone

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:07.640
fractures when you're older.

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:09.015
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, as someone

00:24:09.015 --> 00:24:12.350
who broke his left foot 5
times while in high school,

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.240
I can say that whatever
young people can

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:16.880
do to optimize their bone
density would be great.

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:19.230
That problem seems to have
resolved itself over time,

00:24:19.230 --> 00:24:19.938
but I don't know.

00:24:19.938 --> 00:24:22.790
Back then, I did a short
run as a vegetarian,

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:25.100
but I've always
been an omnivore.

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:26.840
I realize that some
of this relates

00:24:26.840 --> 00:24:29.280
to ethics, and food allergies,
and things of that sort.

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:32.390
But would you say
that, on balance,

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:37.760
most people would benefit from
eating a combination of quality

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:40.550
proteins from animal sources
and non-animal sources--

00:24:40.550 --> 00:24:42.603
fruits, vegetables,
and starches?

00:24:42.603 --> 00:24:44.270
I mean, what do you
think, for instance,

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:48.470
about people following a pure
carnivore or a very pure vegan

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:51.302
diet in their 20s and 30s?

00:24:51.302 --> 00:24:52.760
KYLE GILLETT: In
their late 20s, it

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:54.710
might be a reasonable option.

00:24:54.710 --> 00:24:57.060
In early 20s and
certainly teens,

00:24:57.060 --> 00:25:00.380
it is a horrible idea because
it is likely to significantly

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:02.120
decrease your free androgens.

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:04.190
So you will have less
testosterone acting

00:25:04.190 --> 00:25:06.885
on receptors through the body.

00:25:06.885 --> 00:25:09.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Are there
any other micronutrients

00:25:09.010 --> 00:25:11.740
or macronutrients that
people in their 20s and 30s

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:13.825
should emphasize?

00:25:13.825 --> 00:25:16.450
KYLE GILLETT: We haven't really
touched on fatty acids or fiber

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:17.440
too much.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:20.110
Fiber is going to
be paramount in kind

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:22.090
of setting your set
point of your gut

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:24.440
microbiome the
rest of your life.

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:26.710
There is prebiotic
fiber, which you

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:30.250
can think of as fish food
for your good gut microbiome.

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:32.810
Your gut microbiome is kind
of like an aquarium or a fish

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:33.310
tank.

00:25:33.310 --> 00:25:33.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Now,
I'm just thinking

00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:36.680
about goldfish swimming around
in that-- the goldfish eating

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:37.180
people.

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:38.537
Don't eat goldfish, people--

00:25:38.537 --> 00:25:39.370
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:40.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --live or dead.

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:43.840
KYLE GILLETT: Yeah,
but any fiber or food

00:25:43.840 --> 00:25:45.400
that you're putting
in your gut, it's

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:49.030
either going to skew your gut
microbiome toward something

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:51.933
that is more beneficial
or more detrimental.

00:25:51.933 --> 00:25:53.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And would you say

00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:57.730
that the prebiotic fiber and
getting essential fatty acids,

00:25:57.730 --> 00:26:00.580
that would be important to do
throughout the lifespan or just

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:02.260
for people in their 20s and 30s?

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:03.850
KYLE GILLETT:
Throughout the lifespan,

00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:07.690
particularly important in the
teenage, '20s, '30s, because it

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:09.880
helps with brain development.

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:12.010
You're certainly more
of an expert than me

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:14.650
when it comes to
brain development,

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:17.260
but it does continue
to develop, really,

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:19.510
throughout the lifespan, but
certainly through the 20s

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:20.618
and 30s as well.

00:26:20.618 --> 00:26:22.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
about taking a multivitamin

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:23.660
while you're growing up?

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:25.660
So many people do that.

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:27.620
Is it necessary, is it useful?

00:26:27.620 --> 00:26:31.980
And if it's not necessary,
is it safe to do anyway?

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:34.560
KYLE GILLETT: It's
generally safe to do anyway.

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:37.920
I do not think everybody
needs a multivitamin.

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:40.770
The more exclusionary
your diet is--

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:43.650
for example, if you
have celiac disease

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:46.380
or if you're planning on
fertility soon, then perhaps

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:48.360
it's more reasonable
to take a multivitamin.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:50.443
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In a
previous discussion of ours,

00:26:50.443 --> 00:26:53.280
I asked you about caloric
restriction and testosterone.

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:56.610
And if I recall correctly, the
idea was that if somebody is

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:59.370
overweight-- they have an
excess fat, adipose tissue--

00:26:59.370 --> 00:27:02.280
then getting rid of some
of that adipose tissue

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:04.140
through caloric
restriction and exercise,

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:06.720
provided it's done not
too fast in a healthy way,

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:09.990
is going to be beneficial for
testosterone in the long-run.

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:12.750
But that for
individuals who are not

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:15.060
carrying an excess of body
fat, caloric restriction

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:18.450
is actually going to
lower testosterone.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:20.280
First of all, do I
have that correct?

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:23.820
And second, are there
any addendums to that

00:27:23.820 --> 00:27:26.520
you'd like to give us now?

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:27.840
KYLE GILLETT: That's correct.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:31.890
If you look at an individual
in a caloric deficit,

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:33.490
several changes will happen.

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:37.470
One is that they'll have less
building blocks for hormones.

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:40.110
Another is that they will
be in a catabolic state

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:43.560
more often, so that balance
of anabolism and catabolism

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:44.550
will be different.

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:47.670
They'll likely have less
signaling from growth hormone

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:51.540
and IGF-1, and they'll
also have the high SHBG

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:53.770
that we defined earlier
as the binding protein,

00:27:53.770 --> 00:27:57.750
so their free androgens and
free estrogens will go down.

00:27:57.750 --> 00:27:58.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:01.238
So we touched on
sleep being critical.

00:28:01.238 --> 00:28:03.030
I would say throughout
the lifespan, trying

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:06.240
to get enough quality
sleep at least 80%

00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:08.340
of the nights of your
life, and the other 20%

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.568
are just what happens
when there's noise outside

00:28:11.568 --> 00:28:12.360
or you're stressed.

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:14.220
It just-- you have
an exam or you're

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:16.090
having a great time
for whatever reason.

00:28:16.090 --> 00:28:18.240
There are a lot of good
reasons to lose some sleep

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:20.100
now and again as well.

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:23.160
So we have sleep,
we've got nutrition.

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:24.240
We touched on that.

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:26.455
We'll get back into
supplementation.

00:28:26.455 --> 00:28:28.080
Now, what are some
of the other pillars

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:30.660
of creating the proper
environment for hormone

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:33.350
optimization?

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:35.780
KYLE GILLETT: Stress is
probably the next one.

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:41.270
During both puberty, but
also the 20s and 30s,

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:44.990
individuals are figuring out how
they want to cope with stress

00:28:44.990 --> 00:28:47.390
and also figuring out
what they want to choose

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:50.160
to put their effort into.

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:52.910
So if someone is overstressed,
then it can have--

00:28:52.910 --> 00:28:55.940
it can put all the other
lifestyle pillars--

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:59.870
and then they stop dieting
well, they stop exercising,

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:02.900
and everything
else can go askew.

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:07.920
There is also some degree
of social component to this,

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:12.170
so perhaps I need to add a
seventh pillar of social.

00:29:12.170 --> 00:29:15.500
During your 20s and 30s, you
may be forming a family as well.

00:29:15.500 --> 00:29:19.970
Perhaps you have children, and
the health of the family unit

00:29:19.970 --> 00:29:23.180
is going to be vitally
important not only-- not

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:26.413
necessarily directly for
hormone optimisation,

00:29:26.413 --> 00:29:27.830
but it's going to
throw everything

00:29:27.830 --> 00:29:29.060
else off if it's off.

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:31.368
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
for people that are not

00:29:31.368 --> 00:29:33.410
starting their own families
in their 20s and 30s,

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:36.380
can that social connection
be extended to friendships

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:38.038
and work relationships as well?

00:29:38.038 --> 00:29:39.080
KYLE GILLETT: Absolutely.

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:41.750
In fact, if someone's
not starting a family,

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:44.530
it is just as concerning,
but for other reasons.

00:29:44.530 --> 00:29:47.030
Each individual is going to
have their close group of family

00:29:47.030 --> 00:29:47.880
and friends.

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:50.810
And if someone does not have
one of those connections,

00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:53.120
that's when things
can potentially

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:56.553
get bad not just for them
individually, but also society.

00:29:56.553 --> 00:29:58.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
when you say stress,

00:29:58.220 --> 00:30:00.728
you mean learn to
manage your stress.

00:30:00.728 --> 00:30:01.770
What does that look like?

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:04.610
I mean, if a patient
has high blood pressure,

00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:07.250
or even if they don't, you just
sense that they're stressed.

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:08.750
They have a lot of
pressured speech,

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:10.625
or they're not feeling
well, or communicating

00:30:10.625 --> 00:30:12.080
that they're not doing well.

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:13.580
What are some of
the things that you

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:16.365
recommend in order to try
and ameliorate that stress?

00:30:16.365 --> 00:30:18.740
KYLE GILLETT: There's different
mindfulness or relaxation

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:19.460
techniques.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:23.420
Going outside can often
help with this as well.

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:26.460
Dietary changes and exercise
can help with this too.

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:28.910
Some people like
prayer or meditation.

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:32.660
And a lot of people like
counseling or therapy,

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:36.545
or even just talking openly with
a family member or a friend.

00:30:36.545 --> 00:30:37.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
What would it be

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:40.470
some of the other pillars
for hormone optimization?

00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:41.880
Here, I feel like we're not
just talking about people

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:43.800
in their 20s and
30s, but again, we're

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:47.250
wrapping our arms around
basically puberty onward.

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:49.367
I mean, gosh, looking
back, I started

00:30:49.367 --> 00:30:51.450
meditating pretty early,
I started weight training

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:52.320
and running early.

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:54.882
I gave some thought to
my diet in high school,

00:30:54.882 --> 00:30:56.340
but it really was
in college that I

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:59.340
started thinking more about
what I was ingesting and why

00:30:59.340 --> 00:31:00.880
and trying to do better there.

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:02.580
But people are
coming to the table

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:05.790
at different stages
of life and trying

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:07.190
to optimize for hormones.

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:09.510
So what would be some
of the additional things

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:12.210
that everybody should do?

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:15.720
KYLE GILLETT: Everyone should
get outside and find a movement

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:18.030
pastime to last a lifetime.

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:19.530
You're going to get
sunlight, you're

00:31:19.530 --> 00:31:22.330
going to get some degree
of heat and cold exposure,

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:24.510
and you're also just
going to move more.

00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:27.090
Being in an artificial
environment, where there's

00:31:27.090 --> 00:31:30.480
artificial lights
artificial air conditioning,

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:35.590
is going to have many
effects on your body.

00:31:35.590 --> 00:31:36.750
So that's vital.

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:40.390
Another one is finding what
your purpose is in life.

00:31:40.390 --> 00:31:42.510
So I call this spirit,
but it's really

00:31:42.510 --> 00:31:45.900
just the self-actualization
component of Maslow's hierarchy

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:48.030
of needs, which is basically
your physical needs,

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:50.322
your mental needs, and then
your purpose in life-- what

00:31:50.322 --> 00:31:51.455
you really like to do.

00:31:51.455 --> 00:31:53.580
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
picking some goal or target.

00:31:53.580 --> 00:31:55.290
And I always say that
you don't have to stick

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:56.340
to the same goal over time.

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:57.798
Certainly, I haven't
although I got

00:31:57.798 --> 00:32:00.750
started early in the science
game and I'm still in it.

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:05.170
The idea is not to pick the
end goal, it is to pick a goal.

00:32:05.170 --> 00:32:08.760
And then once you reach that
goal to assess, and then

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:10.240
pick another goal, and so on.

00:32:10.240 --> 00:32:12.030
I think sometimes, when people
hear about picking a purpose,

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:13.500
they're like, oh, my
goodness, I have to define--

00:32:13.500 --> 00:32:15.180
sort of like naming oneself--

00:32:15.180 --> 00:32:19.080
that you actually can change
your goals and purpose

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:20.130
over time.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:21.000
This is terrific.

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:25.200
Would you suggest that
people actively use

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:29.162
or avoid supplementation
prior to doing

00:32:29.162 --> 00:32:30.120
all these other things?

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:32.250
I'm somebody that likes to throw
the kitchen sink at things,

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:34.375
but I also like to do things
pretty systematically.

00:32:34.375 --> 00:32:36.840
So I would say behavior is
first, then nutrition, then

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:40.290
supplementation, and then
maybe if, and only if, there's

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:41.220
a real need--

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:43.740
and of course, working with a
doctor-- prescription drugs.

00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:46.800
But there are probably
people in their 20s or 30s--

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:48.102
maybe even in their 50s--

00:32:48.102 --> 00:32:49.560
that aren't feeling
great, and they

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:52.260
want to do something in order
to be able to train more

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:56.010
or to feel more confident to
seek out social connection.

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:59.010
They just try and go
about the whole business

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:01.480
from the other side as well.

00:33:01.480 --> 00:33:03.142
What are your thoughts on that?

00:33:03.142 --> 00:33:05.100
KYLE GILLETT: I see
supplements and medications

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:06.270
as very similar--

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:08.100
one is prescribed
and one is not.

00:33:08.100 --> 00:33:10.770
In general, medications
have more side effects

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:14.730
or potentially stronger
therapeutic with more efficacy.

00:33:14.730 --> 00:33:17.850
But they are just tools
to reach an end goal.

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:19.680
So depending on the
goal, if there's

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:21.510
an individual that's
an athlete, then

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:24.090
certainly they should
consider supplementation.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:29.330
Or if someone desires
optimal or a very high level

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:31.080
of cognitive performance,
they should also

00:33:31.080 --> 00:33:32.490
consider supplementation.

00:33:32.490 --> 00:33:34.182
At the same time,
food is medicine,

00:33:34.182 --> 00:33:36.390
and a lot of the benefits
you can get in supplements,

00:33:36.390 --> 00:33:37.800
you can get in food as well.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:38.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I guess it depends

00:33:38.430 --> 00:33:40.555
on how much time and energy
you're willing to spend

00:33:40.555 --> 00:33:41.783
and also finances.

00:33:41.783 --> 00:33:43.200
I know that when
I was in college,

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:45.370
I could afford just
a few supplements,

00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:48.953
and they were basically whey
protein and some fish oil--

00:33:48.953 --> 00:33:50.370
I was fortunate
that I was pointed

00:33:50.370 --> 00:33:52.578
in the direction of those
things-- and some creatine.

00:33:52.578 --> 00:33:53.990
I couldn't afford much else.

00:33:53.990 --> 00:33:55.740
Over time, of course,
I could afford more.

00:33:55.740 --> 00:34:00.180
But it really does often
depend on finances.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:02.940
Before we get into some
specific recommendations

00:34:02.940 --> 00:34:06.030
to optimize testosterone,
estrogen, thyroid,

00:34:06.030 --> 00:34:08.130
growth hormone, et
cetera, I want to ask you

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:10.510
a question I've been wondering
about for a long time.

00:34:10.510 --> 00:34:13.409
So often in the discussion
about male hormone optimization,

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:18.710
people will say, well, if
your libido is suffering,

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:21.270
you might want to be
concerned about testosterone--

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:24.120
or even estrogen because we know
that estrogen can impact libido

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:24.690
as well.

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:28.199
Sometimes, having estrogen too
low is detrimental for libido.

00:34:28.199 --> 00:34:30.960
Or that people will say, you're
not recovering from workouts

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:32.670
or you're just you're
feeling kind of depressed.

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:34.170
The problem is,
it's all subjective.

00:34:34.170 --> 00:34:37.800
[CHUCKLES] So how does
one know whether or not

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:41.940
their recovery from workouts,
their energy, their confidence,

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:44.272
their libido is within
a healthy range?

00:34:44.272 --> 00:34:46.230
I mean, obviously, for
people in a relationship

00:34:46.230 --> 00:34:48.300
they can know whether or
not their libido matches

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:51.840
the sort of cadence of the
relationship and their partner.

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:54.607
But how should people
think about this

00:34:54.607 --> 00:34:56.190
and maybe then start
to talk about it?

00:34:56.190 --> 00:34:58.023
Because one of the big
differences, I think,

00:34:58.023 --> 00:35:01.260
between males and females
is that, because females

00:35:01.260 --> 00:35:05.550
have a monthly cycle, they are
familiar with the changes that

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:08.353
occur in their hormones over
time because every 28 days,

00:35:08.353 --> 00:35:10.020
those hormones are
changing dramatically

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:12.390
in ways that impact their
physiology and psychology.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:13.807
But for males, I
feel like there's

00:35:13.807 --> 00:35:19.140
a dearth of language to get
into the more subtle aspects

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:19.680
of this.

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:21.720
It also has to do with
privacy issues and people

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:25.193
feeling like they don't
want to overshare too much--

00:35:25.193 --> 00:35:26.860
not knowing what's
appropriate to share.

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:29.400
But when you talk to a
patient who's in their 30s,

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:31.185
or maybe even their
70s or 60s-- it

00:35:31.185 --> 00:35:34.530
doesn't matter-- a male patient,
what are you listening for?

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:37.750
And I know you're
not a psychiatrist,

00:35:37.750 --> 00:35:40.333
but what are your ears tuned to
in order to try and figure out

00:35:40.333 --> 00:35:41.958
whether or not this
person could really

00:35:41.958 --> 00:35:43.620
use some help with
hormone optimization

00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:44.970
or whether or not
something else--

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:46.200
or maybe they're
just doing great

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:47.908
and they don't realize
it because they're

00:35:47.908 --> 00:35:50.340
placing demands on themselves
that are excessive?

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:53.260
KYLE GILLETT: You want to use
a lot of open-ended questions.

00:35:53.260 --> 00:35:56.070
This process is called
motivational interviewing.

00:35:56.070 --> 00:35:59.190
And your goal is to
listen to the patient

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:02.310
and not plant an
idea in their mind

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:04.950
that they can follow
because everybody is

00:36:04.950 --> 00:36:06.900
going to have a different goal.

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:09.780
Some people are better at
reading their biofeedback

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:14.470
or telling how they
feel on a daily basis.

00:36:14.470 --> 00:36:16.710
There is screening
questionnaires designed--

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:18.480
for example, an
ADAM questionnaire

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:22.240
to look at men's men's health
and hormone-related health.

00:36:22.240 --> 00:36:24.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
called ADAM questionnaire?

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:24.910
KYLE GILLETT: ADAM
questionnaire.

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:25.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A-D-A-M?

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:26.130
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

00:36:26.130 --> 00:36:27.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is
it available online

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:28.950
that people could
administer it to themselves.

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:30.030
Although we don't
want people making

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:32.070
clinical diagnoses of
themselves or anyone else,

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:33.487
is it that sort of exam?

00:36:33.487 --> 00:36:34.320
KYLE GILLETT: It is.

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:35.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:36:35.050 --> 00:36:37.675
KYLE GILLETT: I don't believe it
is a clinically-validated tool

00:36:37.675 --> 00:36:40.950
like an ASCVD, which is like a
risk of heart attack and stroke

00:36:40.950 --> 00:36:43.763
tool or many other tools.

00:36:43.763 --> 00:36:45.930
There's one for depression,
there's one for anxiety.

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:49.630
They're called PHQ9
and GAD7 respectively.

00:36:49.630 --> 00:36:52.830
But anyway, there
is often an in--

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:56.340
the ADAM questionnaire-- and
what you hear from the patient,

00:36:56.340 --> 00:37:00.218
if you are a very careful
listener, is often different.

00:37:00.218 --> 00:37:02.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Can
you give me an example

00:37:02.010 --> 00:37:04.135
of some of the questions
on this ADAM questionnaire

00:37:04.135 --> 00:37:06.870
or the sorts of motivational
interviewing that you might do?

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:08.260
So let's say I'm your patient.

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:08.945
I sit down.

00:37:08.945 --> 00:37:10.320
What sorts of
questions would you

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:13.470
ask to probe these kinds
of dimensions of hormones?

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:15.180
KYLE GILLETT:
Questions about libido,

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:17.310
questions about
athletic performance,

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:19.380
questions about motivation.

00:37:19.380 --> 00:37:22.050
And often, the patient
will answer one thing,

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:25.672
but what you hear from them
subjectively is far different.

00:37:25.672 --> 00:37:26.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:29.280
Can you give me an
example of a question?

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:31.650
I'm happy to be the
guinea pig here.

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:34.710
KYLE GILLETT: A classic
one is, a guy comes in--

00:37:34.710 --> 00:37:36.960
and a lot of times,
they say, oh, no,

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:38.910
the wife made me
go to the doctor.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:40.890
I go once a year, that's it.

00:37:40.890 --> 00:37:43.590
I don't want anything, I
don't want any medications.

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.500
Their screening
questionnaires might be zeros

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:47.850
across the board, so nothing.

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:48.660
No issues.

00:37:48.660 --> 00:37:50.670
They're apparently
in perfect health.

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:53.640
They talk to you for a
while, they get some rapport,

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:56.670
they like you, and then right
as you're finishing up the visit

00:37:56.670 --> 00:37:58.710
and about to go
out the room, they

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:01.890
mention that their
libido isn't quite there,

00:38:01.890 --> 00:38:03.990
and they're having a
little bit of ED as well,

00:38:03.990 --> 00:38:05.760
and perhaps they're
even having some chest

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:06.902
pressure or tightness.

00:38:06.902 --> 00:38:07.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I see.

00:38:07.860 --> 00:38:09.840
So right as you're
leaving the room,

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:12.520
a patient will tell you that
they're having some sexual side

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:13.770
effects-- or not side effects.

00:38:13.770 --> 00:38:15.360
They're having some
sexual challenges,

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:17.880
and then they'll
mention chest pressure.

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:20.220
Is the chest pressure
a general decoy

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:22.955
for, it's got to be
my heart, or is it

00:38:22.955 --> 00:38:25.080
related to the other things
that they're reporting?

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:26.413
KYLE GILLETT: It can be related.

00:38:26.413 --> 00:38:28.830
In fact, erectile
dysfunction is known

00:38:28.830 --> 00:38:31.090
as the canary in the coal mine.

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:33.838
So coal miners would take the
canary down, and it would--

00:38:33.838 --> 00:38:35.880
the canary would die before
the coal miners would

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:38.370
of, I believe, carbon
monoxide poisoning.

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:41.580
And often, one of
the causes of ED

00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:44.040
is plaque buildup, which
can happen in the coronaries

00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:44.760
as well.

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:48.210
But sometimes, they notice
the symptom in the genitals

00:38:48.210 --> 00:38:49.810
before they do in
the coronaries.

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:51.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
for such a patient,

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:54.480
let's say that patient
was a young person where

00:38:54.480 --> 00:38:56.790
plaque buildup in the
arteries and veins

00:38:56.790 --> 00:38:58.980
is not all that likely
if they're, let's say,

00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:01.380
in their 20s or
30s, what would be

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:03.922
your next step of the interview
at that point, and what would

00:39:03.922 --> 00:39:04.463
you consider?

00:39:04.463 --> 00:39:06.540
Would you immediately
order labs for that person

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:10.740
to try and rule out any
kind of actual hormone level

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:11.490
deficiency?

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:13.530
KYLE GILLETT: I certainly
would order labs.

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:15.767
There are some individuals
that are very similar,

00:39:15.767 --> 00:39:17.850
and they come in, and they
have the same symptoms,

00:39:17.850 --> 00:39:21.270
and one individual might have
a very, very high testosterone,

00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:24.990
and one individual might
be severely hypogonadal.

00:39:24.990 --> 00:39:27.810
So there's a big difference
between the subjective

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:29.410
and what the labs look like.

00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:31.200
So I certainly order labs.

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:35.580
You also ask them about if
it's situational or not,

00:39:35.580 --> 00:39:38.087
you ask them if they
have ED if they're--

00:39:38.087 --> 00:39:39.420
you ask them about their habits.

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:42.030
You even ask about
porn, and masturbation,

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:42.955
and all these issues.

00:39:42.955 --> 00:39:45.330
And of course, that's between
the doctor and the patient.

00:39:45.330 --> 00:39:47.100
And depending on
what they tell you,

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:51.270
you can often determine if there
is a situational component--

00:39:51.270 --> 00:39:53.700
some people call
it psychogenic ED,

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:56.100
but I don't love the
term psychogenic ED,

00:39:56.100 --> 00:40:01.060
because it kind of puts some
blame on the patient's mind.

00:40:01.060 --> 00:40:03.270
But a lot of the time,
that is the case.

00:40:03.270 --> 00:40:05.200
There is even a test.

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:07.830
And this is very rarely
ordered, but it's called

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:09.990
a nocturnal penile tumescence.

00:40:09.990 --> 00:40:12.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Now,
is it true that there

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:15.390
are periodic erections
during sleep-- correct?

00:40:15.390 --> 00:40:16.140
KYLE GILLETT: Yes.

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:18.960
So you basically
put a cuff to see

00:40:18.960 --> 00:40:22.920
if you were having a normal
sized erection during sleep.

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:26.520
And I believe about 90% of
the time they do that test,

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:28.380
they are indeed
having erections.

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:29.797
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Which would point

00:40:29.797 --> 00:40:32.533
to this psychogenic
origin of whatever

00:40:32.533 --> 00:40:34.950
challenges they're having in
terms of sexual interactions.

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:37.600
You mentioned porn
and masturbation.

00:40:37.600 --> 00:40:40.563
This topic has come up a
bunch of times on this podcast

00:40:40.563 --> 00:40:41.980
and on other
podcasts I've gone on

00:40:41.980 --> 00:40:45.520
because of the relationship
between dopamine sexual

00:40:45.520 --> 00:40:47.870
motivation and sexual behavior.

00:40:47.870 --> 00:40:51.310
And I've been of the
pretty strong stance

00:40:51.310 --> 00:40:54.760
that, while I'm not judging
porn or masturbation,

00:40:54.760 --> 00:40:58.420
it can create a brain
wiring situation where

00:40:58.420 --> 00:41:01.900
males in particular
essentially teach their brain

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:04.930
to be aroused by watching other
people have sex as opposed

00:41:04.930 --> 00:41:09.470
to being the first person
actor in sexual interactions.

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:13.030
So in that sense, that's
more about the brain wiring,

00:41:13.030 --> 00:41:14.680
and neuroplasticity,
and dopamine.

00:41:14.680 --> 00:41:17.170
But what are your thoughts
on porn and masturbation

00:41:17.170 --> 00:41:18.310
as they relate to hormones?

00:41:18.310 --> 00:41:20.210
I mean, this is a big
debate on the internet.

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:22.390
In fact, one of the
most common debates

00:41:22.390 --> 00:41:25.720
is whether or not masturbation
increases or decreases

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:27.130
testosterone in males.

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:29.980
Certainly, it will
decrease motivation

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:31.870
to go find sexual partners.

00:41:31.870 --> 00:41:32.620
We know this.

00:41:32.620 --> 00:41:33.370
KYLE GILLETT: Yes.

00:41:33.370 --> 00:41:35.745
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And there
are more and more data on this

00:41:35.745 --> 00:41:36.460
all the time.

00:41:36.460 --> 00:41:39.670
In terms of the effects of
pornography and masturbation--

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:42.277
and here, I suppose we need
to be somewhat specific

00:41:42.277 --> 00:41:44.360
and operationally define
what we're talking about.

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:46.690
We're talking about
porn and masturbation

00:41:46.690 --> 00:41:51.280
to the point of ejaculation,
because my understanding

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:55.780
is that the ejaculation
and orgasm associated with

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:57.820
causes an increase in
prolactin, which blunts

00:41:57.820 --> 00:41:59.530
libido for some period of time.

00:41:59.530 --> 00:42:01.930
The duration of that will
vary from person to person

00:42:01.930 --> 00:42:03.490
and circumstance
to circumstance.

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:05.830
But basically, all of
this points to the fact

00:42:05.830 --> 00:42:08.660
that porn and
masturbation can really

00:42:08.660 --> 00:42:11.850
limit libido in the real world.

00:42:11.850 --> 00:42:15.742
And to me, pornography and the
screen is not the real world.

00:42:15.742 --> 00:42:17.450
Although screens exist
in the real world,

00:42:17.450 --> 00:42:19.588
the real world doesn't
exist in the screen.

00:42:19.588 --> 00:42:21.380
KYLE GILLETT: That's
an accurate statement,

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:26.840
and prolactin does have a
significant acute increase

00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:29.400
after ejaculation.

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:33.020
It does to some degree
after orgasm as well,

00:42:33.020 --> 00:42:35.720
but prolactin acts
on the pituitary

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:39.590
to inhibit the release of
the hormones LH and FSH,

00:42:39.590 --> 00:42:42.620
of which LH can
increase testosterone.

00:42:42.620 --> 00:42:44.960
So this may be one
of the cases where

00:42:44.960 --> 00:42:46.400
the dose makes the poison.

00:42:46.400 --> 00:42:48.110
And if it is a very
frequent habit,

00:42:48.110 --> 00:42:52.180
certainly daily or
more than once a day

00:42:52.180 --> 00:42:56.570
would be very detrimental from
a hormonal component not even

00:42:56.570 --> 00:42:58.913
taking into account
the neural wiring.

00:42:58.913 --> 00:43:00.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Listen,
I think it's terrific

00:43:00.830 --> 00:43:02.360
that you've actually
defined frequency,

00:43:02.360 --> 00:43:04.310
because this is the problem
on the internet or even

00:43:04.310 --> 00:43:05.420
in the doctor's office.

00:43:05.420 --> 00:43:07.940
You'll see descriptions
about pornography

00:43:07.940 --> 00:43:09.800
being dangerous
for certain things

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:11.122
or detrimental to hormones.

00:43:11.122 --> 00:43:12.830
People say frequent,
but what's frequent?

00:43:12.830 --> 00:43:15.050
So you're saying daily
or multiple times per day

00:43:15.050 --> 00:43:18.200
would be potentially
detrimental to the hormone

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:20.630
profile of a male of
essentially any age.

00:43:20.630 --> 00:43:23.750
KYLE GILLETT: And that's
just for masturbation.

00:43:23.750 --> 00:43:28.010
With pornography--
with porn use as well,

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:29.845
it would likely be worse.

00:43:29.845 --> 00:43:31.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And why is that?

00:43:31.220 --> 00:43:33.890
Just the sort of
dopaminergic drive

00:43:33.890 --> 00:43:37.010
of the stimulus-- just the
really intense visual stimulus?

00:43:37.010 --> 00:43:38.990
KYLE GILLETT:
Dopamine sensitivity.

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:43.010
I think that using the analogy
of a dopamine wave pool,

00:43:43.010 --> 00:43:46.250
it would deepen the
pool, but not increase

00:43:46.250 --> 00:43:47.600
your supply of dopamine.

00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:49.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Maybe you could
describe the dopamine wave pool

00:43:49.250 --> 00:43:51.440
because I think it's such
a powerful way of thinking

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:53.060
about dopamine and
what dopamine does.

00:43:53.060 --> 00:43:56.600
In fact, I've always credited
you when I've done it,

00:43:56.600 --> 00:44:00.380
but I've generally stolen your
analogy of the dopamine wave

00:44:00.380 --> 00:44:03.110
pool because it's so astute.

00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:04.610
KYLE GILLETT: The
dopamine wave pool

00:44:04.610 --> 00:44:07.940
describes the natural
variation of ups and downs

00:44:07.940 --> 00:44:10.190
in your dopamine
or your motivation.

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:15.920
And in the wave pool, depending
on how high the peak is,

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:20.120
you often have a deeper trough.

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:22.310
So you do not want
to high of a peak.

00:44:22.310 --> 00:44:25.490
In addition, if your
peak is very, very high--

00:44:25.490 --> 00:44:31.490
for example, when you're using
many substances like cocaine

00:44:31.490 --> 00:44:33.110
or like amphetamines,
your dopamine

00:44:33.110 --> 00:44:35.690
can go so high that
you lose almost all

00:44:35.690 --> 00:44:37.400
the water from the
wave pool, and then

00:44:37.400 --> 00:44:39.920
when you crash from that,
not only is the trough low,

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:43.370
you have less dopamine in
the pool to begin with.

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:45.230
The dopamine
receptor is extremely

00:44:45.230 --> 00:44:47.480
sensitive, as is the
Gaba receptor, which

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:50.270
is an inhibitory receptor,
whereas dopamine is technically

00:44:50.270 --> 00:44:53.960
a stimulant more related to
adrenaline and noradrenaline.

00:44:53.960 --> 00:44:57.960
The depth of the pool
can change very quick.

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:00.110
So you want to have that
happy medium where you're

00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:03.110
fairly near the top, but
you're not so near the top

00:45:03.110 --> 00:45:06.050
that the depth of the
pool is going to go down.

00:45:06.050 --> 00:45:09.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So if I
interpret that in the context

00:45:09.430 --> 00:45:14.740
of this discussion about libido,
sex, porn, and masturbation,

00:45:14.740 --> 00:45:19.030
if somebody has a very intense
