Even if you're maintaining
adequate protein intake,

00:55:05.990 --> 00:55:08.720
you might not be maintaining
adequate iron intake

00:55:08.720 --> 00:55:12.380
or adequate vitamin D. And
you're also just literally

00:55:12.380 --> 00:55:13.700
in a caloric deficit.

00:55:13.700 --> 00:55:17.750
Perhaps low carbs as well,
very low free testosterone.

00:55:17.750 --> 00:55:23.022
And they're simultaneously doing
a lot of vigorous exercise.

00:55:23.022 --> 00:55:24.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:55:24.230 --> 00:55:27.140
I often hear, and I'm starting
to wonder whether or not,

00:55:27.140 --> 00:55:33.780
some of the quicker-to-results
nutrition tactics--

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:36.200
things like dropping
all carbohydrates,

00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:39.890
or the quicker-to-results
exercise habits,

00:55:39.890 --> 00:55:42.050
like starting to do six
day a week really intense

00:55:42.050 --> 00:55:44.758
workouts-- whether or
not, in the short run,

00:55:44.758 --> 00:55:47.300
they work because they cause
the cosmetic changes that people

00:55:47.300 --> 00:55:49.640
are seeking, but that
they really undermine

00:55:49.640 --> 00:55:52.670
the overall goal, which
is, at least to me,

00:55:52.670 --> 00:55:57.560
to have your hormones Maybe.
not optimized to 100%,

00:55:57.560 --> 00:56:00.620
but to always be aiming
for 100% and be close to it

00:56:00.620 --> 00:56:02.390
at every stage of life.

00:56:02.390 --> 00:56:04.400
KYLE GILLETT:
Consistency is key here.

00:56:04.400 --> 00:56:07.460
If you are not
consistent, then the law

00:56:07.460 --> 00:56:09.690
of diminishing returns
certainly applies.

00:56:09.690 --> 00:56:15.590
So 80% or 90% of the benefit
over many, many months

00:56:15.590 --> 00:56:20.373
is far better than 100%,
but only half the time.

00:56:20.373 --> 00:56:21.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:56:21.290 --> 00:56:23.510
One thing that I found
to be tremendously useful

00:56:23.510 --> 00:56:27.590
is to finish the workout while
I still have energy, to not take

00:56:27.590 --> 00:56:28.940
myself to exhaustion.

00:56:28.940 --> 00:56:31.610
And then I'm able to talk
about the dopamine wave pool.

00:56:31.610 --> 00:56:33.710
I'm able to sort of
ride that into the rest

00:56:33.710 --> 00:56:34.850
of the day feeling great.

00:56:34.850 --> 00:56:39.140
I sort of save or bank some
of the vigor from the training

00:56:39.140 --> 00:56:40.380
to bring it into my work.

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:41.838
But then again,
I'm not an athlete.

00:56:41.838 --> 00:56:44.930
I get paid to think and to
speak, not to lift weights

00:56:44.930 --> 00:56:45.495
or to run.

00:56:45.495 --> 00:56:47.120
KYLE GILLETT: Another
component of that

00:56:47.120 --> 00:56:49.460
is the balance between
your sympathetic,

00:56:49.460 --> 00:56:51.350
which is your fight or
flight nervous system,

00:56:51.350 --> 00:56:53.930
and your parasympathetic,
which is your rest or digest

00:56:53.930 --> 00:56:55.310
nervous system.

00:56:55.310 --> 00:56:57.950
There is an anecdote,
which is likely true,

00:56:57.950 --> 00:57:00.530
that many elite
bodybuilders are very

00:57:00.530 --> 00:57:03.177
parasympathetic besides while
they're lifting weights.

00:57:03.177 --> 00:57:04.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You
mean they're lazy

00:57:04.760 --> 00:57:05.840
and they like to eat a lot.

00:57:05.840 --> 00:57:06.170
KYLE GILLETT: Yeah.

00:57:06.170 --> 00:57:08.712
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The lazy bear
in the gym kind of phenomenon.

00:57:08.712 --> 00:57:09.980
KYLE GILLETT: Absolutely.

00:57:09.980 --> 00:57:13.798
But that being said, after a
very, very vigorous workout--

00:57:13.798 --> 00:57:15.840
for example, one where
you're trained to failure,

00:57:15.840 --> 00:57:18.780
which bodybuilders and power
lifters do all the time--

00:57:18.780 --> 00:57:24.020
you feel the tiredness--
or you feel the strain

00:57:24.020 --> 00:57:27.050
from that heavy
sympathetic activity

00:57:27.050 --> 00:57:28.880
when you are lifting
a heavy weight,

00:57:28.880 --> 00:57:31.610
and it can
potentially affect how

00:57:31.610 --> 00:57:32.880
you feel the rest of the day.

00:57:32.880 --> 00:57:37.490
So many people who have a job
where that is highly cognitive

00:57:37.490 --> 00:57:39.755
do not like to have an
extremely vigorous workout

00:57:39.755 --> 00:57:41.630
in the morning, which
is when a lot of people

00:57:41.630 --> 00:57:42.893
are able to exercise.

00:57:42.893 --> 00:57:45.560
ANDREW HUBERMAN: When I exercise
early in the morning-- that is,

00:57:45.560 --> 00:57:46.460
before 9:00 AM--

00:57:46.460 --> 00:57:48.020
I have more energy all day long.

00:57:48.020 --> 00:57:49.820
If I do it mid-morning,
I have experienced

00:57:49.820 --> 00:57:51.050
more of an afternoon crash.

00:57:51.050 --> 00:57:53.760
There's probably some
circadian biology in there.

00:57:53.760 --> 00:57:56.810
I also noticed-- and I've
actually seen in my bloodwork--

00:57:56.810 --> 00:58:00.110
that if I don't get
out for a 45-minute jog

00:58:00.110 --> 00:58:03.470
at least once a week,
all of my blood profiles

00:58:03.470 --> 00:58:05.860
suffer in a direction that
I don't want them to go.

00:58:05.860 --> 00:58:08.620
In particular, testosterone
and estrogen move in directions

00:58:08.620 --> 00:58:12.550
that are not
conducive to my goals.

00:58:12.550 --> 00:58:15.070
I'd like to talk about
some of the approaches

00:58:15.070 --> 00:58:17.440
that people can use in
order to optimize hormones.

00:58:17.440 --> 00:58:20.260
And these days, for
better or for worse--

00:58:20.260 --> 00:58:22.090
I think for worse--

00:58:22.090 --> 00:58:26.560
younger guys are
asking about and using

00:58:26.560 --> 00:58:29.220
testosterone replacement
therapy-- so-called TRT.

00:58:29.220 --> 00:58:30.970
And I just want to
frame this up by saying

00:58:30.970 --> 00:58:33.310
there is no strict
cutoff for what is TRT.

00:58:33.310 --> 00:58:36.950
There are plenty of people whose
blood levels of testosterone

00:58:36.950 --> 00:58:39.340
and estrogen are within
the normal reference range

00:58:39.340 --> 00:58:41.020
and decide to start
doing these things.

00:58:41.020 --> 00:58:42.560
Of course, they can
limit fertility.

00:58:42.560 --> 00:58:45.040
There are a bunch of
issues even at non, quote,

00:58:45.040 --> 00:58:49.180
unquote, steroidal
performance enhancing dosages.

00:58:49.180 --> 00:58:51.490
I'd love to frame this up
by first defining our terms,

00:58:51.490 --> 00:58:53.477
because one of the
challenges on the internet

00:58:53.477 --> 00:58:55.060
is people talk about
TRT, then they'll

00:58:55.060 --> 00:58:56.440
talk about performance
enhancing drugs,

00:58:56.440 --> 00:58:57.607
they'll talk about steroids.

00:58:57.607 --> 00:58:59.260
They're all steroids.

00:58:59.260 --> 00:59:02.440
I mean, testosterone, estrogen
are both steroid hormones.

00:59:02.440 --> 00:59:05.740
But what one considers
replacement therapy

00:59:05.740 --> 00:59:08.740
versus what one considers
performance enhancing

00:59:08.740 --> 00:59:10.210
is going to depend.

00:59:10.210 --> 00:59:13.310
So here's my question.

00:59:13.310 --> 00:59:14.860
Why in the world--

00:59:14.860 --> 00:59:17.200
[EMPHASIS] why in the world--

00:59:17.200 --> 00:59:23.470
would any male in his teens
or twenties, or even 30s,

00:59:23.470 --> 00:59:26.447
whose blood levels of
testosterone and estrogen

00:59:26.447 --> 00:59:28.030
are at the appropriate
levels, meaning

00:59:28.030 --> 00:59:30.970
within the normal
reference range,

00:59:30.970 --> 00:59:35.470
take exogenous testosterone
given all the negative effects

00:59:35.470 --> 00:59:38.170
on fertility, some of
the challenges that it

00:59:38.170 --> 00:59:40.750
can present if the dosages
aren't quite right, et cetera.

00:59:40.750 --> 00:59:43.000
Why would they do
that, certainly

00:59:43.000 --> 00:59:47.030
if they are not being paid
for a particular endeavor,

00:59:47.030 --> 00:59:48.280
like they're not making money?

00:59:48.280 --> 00:59:49.510
If they are playing
a sport, chances

00:59:49.510 --> 00:59:51.260
are they're not allowed
to do that anyway.

00:59:51.260 --> 00:59:53.110
It's on the banned
substances list.

00:59:53.110 --> 00:59:57.040
So to me, it just seems
like a crazy idea.

00:59:57.040 --> 00:59:58.540
But then again,
I'm of a generation

00:59:58.540 --> 01:00:01.360
that really hasn't thought about
doing that stuff until people

01:00:01.360 --> 01:00:04.690
were in their 40s and
50s or even never.

01:00:04.690 --> 01:00:08.470
So is there ever a case for
somebody in their 20s or 30s

01:00:08.470 --> 01:00:11.770
to take testosterone if
their blood levels are

01:00:11.770 --> 01:00:16.630
within the 300 to 900 nanograms
per deciliter reference range?

01:00:16.630 --> 01:00:18.250
KYLE GILLETT: Not many cases.

01:00:18.250 --> 01:00:21.790
The reason for any performance
enhancing drug, whether or not

01:00:21.790 --> 01:00:26.980
it is a steroid, synthetic,
bioidentical, or otherwise,

01:00:26.980 --> 01:00:27.850
varies a lot.

01:00:27.850 --> 01:00:31.990
Some individuals do it
only for cosmetic reasons

01:00:31.990 --> 01:00:35.590
even if it can have
deleterious effects

01:00:35.590 --> 01:00:38.650
on the cosmetic
appearance, for example,

01:00:38.650 --> 01:00:40.660
of your skin in the long run.

01:00:40.660 --> 01:00:44.770
But everyone has their
different reasons.

01:00:44.770 --> 01:00:47.740
As far as like when
does the benefit

01:00:47.740 --> 01:00:52.120
outweigh the detriment, not
very often if you're in your 20s

01:00:52.120 --> 01:00:53.350
and certainly--

01:00:53.350 --> 01:00:55.000
probably-- almost hardly never.

01:00:55.000 --> 01:00:58.660
There's always rare cases, like
Coleman syndrome and whatnot,

01:00:58.660 --> 01:01:02.437
but almost never if
you're very young.

01:01:02.437 --> 01:01:05.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So for people
in their 20s, 30s, and beyond--

01:01:05.020 --> 01:01:08.680
40s, et cetera-- whose
testosterone and estrogen

01:01:08.680 --> 01:01:10.600
levels are at the
appropriate ratios,

01:01:10.600 --> 01:01:13.330
and then within the
normal reference range,

01:01:13.330 --> 01:01:14.800
and they feel pretty good--

01:01:14.800 --> 01:01:17.133
[CHUCKLES] we talked about
the ADAMs exam-- or this sort

01:01:17.133 --> 01:01:20.620
of like feel-pretty-good is code
for libido, energy, recovery,

01:01:20.620 --> 01:01:23.920
et cetera, and are
feeling at least

01:01:23.920 --> 01:01:26.590
workable for their lifestyle.

01:01:26.590 --> 01:01:29.710
For those people,
what can they do

01:01:29.710 --> 01:01:33.280
besides get great sleep, train,
but not too hard or too often,

01:01:33.280 --> 01:01:34.180
et cetera, et cetera?

01:01:34.180 --> 01:01:36.670
What are some of the things in
the realm of supplementation

01:01:36.670 --> 01:01:40.540
that can help them optimize
their testosterone and estrogen

01:01:40.540 --> 01:01:44.410
without suppressing their
own endogenous production

01:01:44.410 --> 01:01:46.600
of testosterone and estrogen?

01:01:46.600 --> 01:01:49.090
KYLE GILLETT: Let's mention
creatine is the first one.

01:01:49.090 --> 01:01:50.678
Creatine is
interesting because it

01:01:50.678 --> 01:01:51.970
has multiple different effects.

01:01:51.970 --> 01:01:54.220
It helps with amino
acid synthesis.

01:01:54.220 --> 01:01:56.870
It also helps with
oxidative stress.

01:01:56.870 --> 01:01:58.810
It can also serve
as the backup fuel

01:01:58.810 --> 01:02:02.380
tank for your mitochondria,
so holding backup ATP.

01:02:02.380 --> 01:02:05.740
And it does slightly
increase total testosterone,

01:02:05.740 --> 01:02:08.440
and it also increases the
conversion of testosterone

01:02:08.440 --> 01:02:11.170
to dihydrotestosterone,
so potentially it's

01:02:11.170 --> 01:02:16.180
especially useful in men
in even their teenage years

01:02:16.180 --> 01:02:16.788
and their 20s.

01:02:16.788 --> 01:02:19.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You mentioned
the conversion of testosterone

01:02:19.330 --> 01:02:22.510
to dihydrotestosterone, and
there is mythology out there

01:02:22.510 --> 01:02:24.190
that creatine can
increase hair loss,

01:02:24.190 --> 01:02:26.590
I'm guessing because there's
at least one study showing

01:02:26.590 --> 01:02:28.840
that creatine can increase DHT--

01:02:28.840 --> 01:02:29.830
dihydrotestosterone.

01:02:29.830 --> 01:02:33.460
And DHT is one of the
primary hormones that can

01:02:33.460 --> 01:02:35.620
promote male pattern baldness.

01:02:35.620 --> 01:02:41.200
So the question therefore is,
does creatine supplementation

01:02:41.200 --> 01:02:44.030
increase the rate of hair loss?

01:02:44.030 --> 01:02:50.920
KYLE GILLETT: Theoretically,
it can, but in each individual,

01:02:50.920 --> 01:02:53.530
preventing hair loss
is a very poor reason

01:02:53.530 --> 01:02:55.000
to take creatine,
because it's not

01:02:55.000 --> 01:02:58.360
going to take you to a
supraphysiologic level,

01:02:58.360 --> 01:03:03.070
it's not going to
increase your androgens

01:03:03.070 --> 01:03:06.670
to a unnormal level of binding.

01:03:06.670 --> 01:03:08.980
So I feel like this--

01:03:08.980 --> 01:03:12.430
if that was a reason to not
take creatine for hair loss,

01:03:12.430 --> 01:03:13.180
then that's--

01:03:13.180 --> 01:03:14.638
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
You mean-- sorry--

01:03:14.638 --> 01:03:17.290
hair loss is not a reason
to avoid taking creatine?

01:03:17.290 --> 01:03:18.207
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

01:03:18.207 --> 01:03:21.520
Hair loss is not a reason
to avoid taking creatine.

01:03:21.520 --> 01:03:24.760
Think of it as just
bringing you to what you

01:03:24.760 --> 01:03:27.610
are naturally inclined to have.

01:03:27.610 --> 01:03:30.190
If your conversion of
testosterone to DHT

01:03:30.190 --> 01:03:34.480
is already high, then often,
creatine does not affect this.

01:03:34.480 --> 01:03:36.640
It just kind of
resets your balance

01:03:36.640 --> 01:03:39.790
between testosterone being
aromatized to estrogen

01:03:39.790 --> 01:03:42.880
or being 5-alpha reduced DHT.

01:03:42.880 --> 01:03:46.120
So it's not going to speed
up hair loss more than just

01:03:46.120 --> 01:03:47.890
naturally being a male does.

01:03:47.890 --> 01:03:50.620
So in some individuals,
it will have no effect.

01:03:50.620 --> 01:03:52.840
In some individuals--
for whatever reason,

01:03:52.840 --> 01:03:55.570
they have almost no 5-alpha
reductase activity--

01:03:55.570 --> 01:03:57.732
it will return them
to natural or normal.

01:03:57.732 --> 01:03:58.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I see.

01:03:58.690 --> 01:04:01.510
Well, I take 5 grams a day
of creatine monohydrate.

01:04:01.510 --> 01:04:04.540
I do it for the
tissue volumizing

01:04:04.540 --> 01:04:06.460
effects, so for
exercise benefits,

01:04:06.460 --> 01:04:08.110
but also for the
cognitive effects.

01:04:08.110 --> 01:04:10.030
I don't know if it's
increasing my hair loss.

01:04:10.030 --> 01:04:13.420
I mean, I've got a little bit
of widow's peak type hair loss.

01:04:13.420 --> 01:04:15.730
That's where it is for me.

01:04:15.730 --> 01:04:18.850
I suppose beard growth is
associated with DHT too.

01:04:18.850 --> 01:04:19.720
Is that right?

01:04:19.720 --> 01:04:20.050
KYLE GILLETT: Yes

01:04:20.050 --> 01:04:20.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
What I learned--

01:04:20.710 --> 01:04:22.835
but then again I haven't
been into those literature

01:04:22.835 --> 01:04:25.780
in a long time-- is that because
of differences in receptors,

01:04:25.780 --> 01:04:28.810
that DHT causes hair
growth on the face and hair

01:04:28.810 --> 01:04:29.710
loss on the head.

01:04:29.710 --> 01:04:30.520
Is that right?

01:04:30.520 --> 01:04:32.080
KYLE GILLETT: Yes,
and the amount,

01:04:32.080 --> 01:04:35.110
and the sensitivity, and
density of those receptors

01:04:35.110 --> 01:04:37.140
is genetically determined.

01:04:37.140 --> 01:04:39.640
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And is it true
that if your mother's father

01:04:39.640 --> 01:04:42.590
was bald that you will be
bald in the same pattern,

01:04:42.590 --> 01:04:45.280
and if he wasn't, you won't?

01:04:45.280 --> 01:04:47.620
KYLE GILLETT: That is
a decent correlation.

01:04:47.620 --> 01:04:49.675
Part of the proposed
mechanism of this-- well,

01:04:49.675 --> 01:04:51.550
there are several genes,
and you can actually

01:04:51.550 --> 01:04:53.630
test your genes for hair loss.

01:04:53.630 --> 01:04:56.260
You do get a decent amount
of them from your mother.

01:04:56.260 --> 01:04:58.270
The unique thing you
get from your mother

01:04:58.270 --> 01:05:00.410
that she may have
gotten from her father--

01:05:00.410 --> 01:05:02.410
that she got one of the
copies from her father--

01:05:02.410 --> 01:05:03.760
is your X chromosome.

01:05:03.760 --> 01:05:07.640
And the androgen receptor
gene is on your X chromosome,

01:05:07.640 --> 01:05:10.810
so all men got their
androgen receptor

01:05:10.810 --> 01:05:12.337
gene from their mother.

01:05:12.337 --> 01:05:14.170
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
on their X chromosome,

01:05:14.170 --> 01:05:15.263
not on the Y chromosome.

01:05:15.263 --> 01:05:16.180
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

01:05:16.180 --> 01:05:17.388
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

01:05:17.388 --> 01:05:19.270
Even though all of
the, quote, unquote,

01:05:19.270 --> 01:05:24.130
male promoting genes are on
the Y chromosome, like malaria,

01:05:24.130 --> 01:05:25.480
and inhibiting, et cetera.

01:05:25.480 --> 01:05:26.770
Interesting.

01:05:26.770 --> 01:05:30.700
So 5 grams a day of creatine
for most people should be fine.

01:05:30.700 --> 01:05:33.490
Beneficial for tissue
volumizing-- so strength,

01:05:33.490 --> 01:05:35.080
bringing water
into the muscles--

01:05:35.080 --> 01:05:37.900
and for the cognitive effects.

01:05:37.900 --> 01:05:40.030
And the clinical support
for creatine, I think,

01:05:40.030 --> 01:05:42.970
is quite strong at the
5 gram per day dosage.

01:05:42.970 --> 01:05:46.130
What other sorts of supplements
can people benefit from?

01:05:46.130 --> 01:05:47.560
We already talked
about the omegas

01:05:47.560 --> 01:05:50.500
and making sure that people are
getting enough prebiotic fiber

01:05:50.500 --> 01:05:54.010
to support the gut
microbiome and vitamin D.

01:05:54.010 --> 01:05:57.790
So what other supplement-based
tools can people consider?

01:05:57.790 --> 01:06:00.040
KYLE GILLETT: Another one
we can loop in with creatine

01:06:00.040 --> 01:06:01.030
is betaine.

01:06:01.030 --> 01:06:03.460
Some people are
nonresponders to creatine,

01:06:03.460 --> 01:06:05.890
so you can increase
that to 10 grams,

01:06:05.890 --> 01:06:07.960
or you can use
its cousin betaine

01:06:07.960 --> 01:06:10.030
to help with amino
acid synthesis

01:06:10.030 --> 01:06:12.400
and shunting of energy.

01:06:12.400 --> 01:06:16.930
Along with that, I would put
l-carnitine which is actually

01:06:16.930 --> 01:06:18.310
the smallest peptide hormone.

01:06:18.310 --> 01:06:21.310
It's just two amino acids that
are put together, so it's a--

01:06:21.310 --> 01:06:23.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's a hormone?

01:06:23.080 --> 01:06:23.862
Interesting.

01:06:23.862 --> 01:06:24.820
I'm not challenging it.

01:06:24.820 --> 01:06:25.120
KYLE GILLETT: Well, peptide.

01:06:25.120 --> 01:06:25.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm just--

01:06:25.900 --> 01:06:26.560
I'm not challenging it.

01:06:26.560 --> 01:06:29.270
KYLE GILLETT: I would call it
a peptide more than a hormone.

01:06:29.270 --> 01:06:31.810
So I would not call
l-carnitine a hormone,

01:06:31.810 --> 01:06:33.850
but I would call
dopamine a hormone.

01:06:33.850 --> 01:06:36.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I
could-- a neurohormone.

01:06:36.060 --> 01:06:39.130
It's so hard to define things
as transmitters or hormones

01:06:39.130 --> 01:06:39.910
at some level.

01:06:39.910 --> 01:06:40.720
I agree.

01:06:40.720 --> 01:06:43.570
So l-carnitine-- actually,
I should backtrack.

01:06:43.570 --> 01:06:48.490
Betaine-- do you recall
what dosage people typically

01:06:48.490 --> 01:06:51.430
would take if they're a
creatine non-responder?

01:06:51.430 --> 01:06:52.555
KYLE GILLETT: 1 to 3 grams.

01:06:52.555 --> 01:06:52.870
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Per day?

01:06:52.870 --> 01:06:53.828
KYLE GILLETT: In fact--

01:06:53.828 --> 01:06:55.330
yeah-- several
versions of creatine

01:06:55.330 --> 01:06:59.080
have betaine mixed in because
it helps with the processing

01:06:59.080 --> 01:07:00.713
of methionine and homocysteine.

01:07:00.713 --> 01:07:03.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So if somebody
is already taking creatine

01:07:03.130 --> 01:07:04.720
and likes it and
responds to it--

01:07:04.720 --> 01:07:06.490
I'll raise my hand--
such as myself,

01:07:06.490 --> 01:07:10.853
would adding betaine help or
is it redundant with creatine?

01:07:10.853 --> 01:07:12.520
KYLE GILLETT: Only
if their homocysteine

01:07:12.520 --> 01:07:13.780
is persistently elevated.

01:07:13.780 --> 01:07:16.420
And homocysteine is kind of
like an inflammatory marker that

01:07:16.420 --> 01:07:18.850
can build up if you're not
converting enough of it

01:07:18.850 --> 01:07:19.660
down stream.

01:07:19.660 --> 01:07:21.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
How would I know?

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:23.560
KYLE GILLETT: Just a
blood test or if you

01:07:23.560 --> 01:07:26.140
knew your MTHFR
polymorphism, which

01:07:26.140 --> 01:07:29.710
is basically how you
add methyl groups

01:07:29.710 --> 01:07:31.632
to many things in the body.

01:07:31.632 --> 01:07:32.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

01:07:32.590 --> 01:07:36.280
Any side effects of betaine
that people should be aware of?

01:07:36.280 --> 01:07:37.660
KYLE GILLETT: Not
that I know of.

01:07:37.660 --> 01:07:39.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: People can
look it up on-- examine.com

01:07:39.730 --> 01:07:40.772
is a great site for that.

01:07:40.772 --> 01:07:41.740
They'll surely list it.

01:07:41.740 --> 01:07:43.448
They just revamped
their site by the way,

01:07:43.448 --> 01:07:48.280
and it was awesome before,
and it's platinum now.

01:07:48.280 --> 01:07:51.820
So l-carnitine-- what are
the ways to take l-carnitine?

01:07:51.820 --> 01:07:54.070
I know that there is
oral forms in capsules

01:07:54.070 --> 01:07:55.160
and there's injectables.

01:07:55.160 --> 01:07:57.250
The injectables, I think
you need a prescription.

01:07:57.250 --> 01:07:58.000
Is that right?

01:07:58.000 --> 01:07:58.510
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

01:07:58.510 --> 01:08:00.280
You need a prescription
for the injectables,

01:08:00.280 --> 01:08:01.905
or you should really
get a prescription

01:08:01.905 --> 01:08:03.610
for the injectables.

01:08:03.610 --> 01:08:06.190
For when you inject it--

01:08:06.190 --> 01:08:08.170
of course, at the
supervision of your doctor--

01:08:08.170 --> 01:08:10.060
it's usually done
intramuscularly.

01:08:10.060 --> 01:08:12.490
It's an aqueous
solution, so it does not

01:08:12.490 --> 01:08:14.380
have an oil or a
carrier oil in it

01:08:14.380 --> 01:08:17.750
like TR-- like
testosterone esters do.

01:08:17.750 --> 01:08:20.682
However, if you inject
it too superficially,

01:08:20.682 --> 01:08:22.390
it's not going to make
or break anything.

01:08:22.390 --> 01:08:25.420
Often, it just burns if you
inject it subcutaneously

01:08:25.420 --> 01:08:29.380
and it does not disseminate
throughout the body as well.

01:08:29.380 --> 01:08:32.920
L-carnitine potentially
has localized effects

01:08:32.920 --> 01:08:34.359
if you inject it.

01:08:34.359 --> 01:08:38.050
If you ingest it orally,
then it has a very low

01:08:38.050 --> 01:08:39.793
bioavailability--
maybe only 10%.

01:08:39.793 --> 01:08:41.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
I think most people are

01:08:41.710 --> 01:08:43.630
going to be able to
get l-carnitine only--

01:08:43.630 --> 01:08:46.220
or in its capsule form.

01:08:46.220 --> 01:08:47.979
So what are the
dosages of l-carnitine

01:08:47.979 --> 01:08:50.200
that one needs to
ingest then if they

01:08:50.200 --> 01:08:54.880
want to get a benefit because
if only 10% is being absorbed,

01:08:54.880 --> 01:08:56.485
it's probably a
lot of l-carnitine.

01:08:56.485 --> 01:08:58.000
How much should
people take per day?

01:08:58.000 --> 01:08:59.500
KYLE GILLETT:
Usually, I recommend

01:08:59.500 --> 01:09:02.890
for oral l-carnitine
between 1,000 milligrams

01:09:02.890 --> 01:09:05.680
and up to 4,000 or
5,000 milligrams.

01:09:05.680 --> 01:09:08.543
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So 1 to
4-- maybe even 5 grams.

01:09:08.543 --> 01:09:09.460
KYLE GILLETT: Correct.

01:09:09.460 --> 01:09:10.960
Up to 5 grams a day.

01:09:10.960 --> 01:09:13.060
If you're on that
much, especially

01:09:13.060 --> 01:09:15.850
if you have a dysregulated
gut microbiome,

01:09:15.850 --> 01:09:17.859
you should be
concerned with TMAO

01:09:17.859 --> 01:09:20.830
which is a potential
carcinogen that both carnitine

01:09:20.830 --> 01:09:23.080
and choline can convert into.

01:09:23.080 --> 01:09:25.540
And your gut
microbiota determine

01:09:25.540 --> 01:09:26.763
how much that happens.

01:09:26.763 --> 01:09:28.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Is it true that I

01:09:28.180 --> 01:09:31.630
can offset any negative
effects of alpha GPC--

01:09:31.630 --> 01:09:34.750
choline that is-- and
l-carnitine that I

01:09:34.750 --> 01:09:36.670
take by ingesting garlic?

01:09:36.670 --> 01:09:37.330
Is that right?

01:09:37.330 --> 01:09:39.880
KYLE GILLETT: There's a compound
in garlic called allicin--

01:09:39.880 --> 01:09:42.790
I believe it's
A-L-L-I-C-I-N. It's also part

01:09:42.790 --> 01:09:46.899
of the scientific name-- the
genus of types of garlic.

01:09:46.899 --> 01:09:49.990
And this can help decrease
the conversion to TMAO.

01:09:49.990 --> 01:09:52.390
Berberine actually slightly
decreases the conversion

01:09:52.390 --> 01:09:55.420
to TMAO as well probably
through alteration

01:09:55.420 --> 01:09:56.680
of the gut microbiome.

01:09:56.680 --> 01:09:59.370
And then just optimizing
your gut microbiome

01:09:59.370 --> 01:10:00.370
can decrease conversion.

01:10:00.370 --> 01:10:03.003
So not everyone needs
allicin, but it's something

01:10:03.003 --> 01:10:04.420
that you should
certainly consider

01:10:04.420 --> 01:10:06.377
if you were on a high dose.

01:10:06.377 --> 01:10:07.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm
going to continue

01:10:07.960 --> 01:10:10.930
to take the 600 milligrams
of garlic every time

01:10:10.930 --> 01:10:12.580
I take my l-carnitine,
but I'm going

01:10:12.580 --> 01:10:14.320
to skip the berberine
because berberine gives me

01:10:14.320 --> 01:10:16.945
brutal headaches and it makes me
crave carbohydrates because it

01:10:16.945 --> 01:10:17.862
drops my blood sugar.

01:10:17.862 --> 01:10:20.320
KYLE GILLETT: It has many other
effects, including the dawn

01:10:20.320 --> 01:10:21.820
phenomenon where
it drops your blood

01:10:21.820 --> 01:10:24.205
sugar when you're sleeping
and you can't even realize it.

01:10:24.205 --> 01:10:26.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I am
not a fan of berberine,

01:10:26.080 --> 01:10:28.300
and I'm sorry for
those of you that are.

01:10:28.300 --> 01:10:30.860
I'm not trying to offend
anyone, although frankly,

01:10:30.860 --> 01:10:33.670
if you're being offended
by my stance on berberine,

01:10:33.670 --> 01:10:35.650
then maybe we should
have another discussion.

01:10:35.650 --> 01:10:38.260
In any case injectable
l-carnitine,

01:10:38.260 --> 01:10:41.500
if one can get that through a
doctor, how much is absorbed

01:10:41.500 --> 01:10:43.350
and how much should one take?

01:10:43.350 --> 01:10:45.100
KYLE GILLETT: Almost
all of it's absorbed.

01:10:45.100 --> 01:10:49.300
In general you're taking
between 500 milligrams up to--

01:10:49.300 --> 01:10:52.600
you can take a pretty high
dose up to 2,000 milligrams.

01:10:52.600 --> 01:10:54.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And what
we did not talk about

01:10:54.670 --> 01:10:56.560
is what l-carnitine does?

01:10:56.560 --> 01:10:59.050
So why should why should
someone go through all of this?

01:10:59.050 --> 01:11:01.660
Is it to optimize
testosterone, is it

01:11:01.660 --> 01:11:03.300
working on the receptor side?

01:11:03.300 --> 01:11:05.162
What's l-carnitine doing?

01:11:05.162 --> 01:11:06.370
KYLE GILLETT: It's a shuttle.

01:11:06.370 --> 01:11:10.270
So I think it's named
carnitine palmitoyl coenzyme

01:11:10.270 --> 01:11:13.450
A. Basically, it
just takes nutrients

01:11:13.450 --> 01:11:16.340
from outside your
mitochondria and puts them in.

01:11:16.340 --> 01:11:18.100
It also has a unique effect--

01:11:18.100 --> 01:11:20.230
well, not too unique
because tadalafil actually

01:11:20.230 --> 01:11:21.490
has this effect as well--

01:11:21.490 --> 01:11:25.180
is that it increases the
density of the androgen receptor

01:11:25.180 --> 01:11:27.980
and the cytoplasm of your cells.

01:11:27.980 --> 01:11:30.460
So even if your androgen
receptor sensitivity

01:11:30.460 --> 01:11:34.000
doesn't change and even if your
testosterone does not change,

01:11:34.000 --> 01:11:36.940
you will have more testosterone
binding to that increased

01:11:36.940 --> 01:11:37.865
number of receptors.

01:11:37.865 --> 01:11:39.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Does
one need to cycle

01:11:39.490 --> 01:11:41.870
l-carnitine, creatine, betaine?

01:11:41.870 --> 01:11:45.105
KYLE GILLETT: No reason
to cycle any of those.

01:11:45.105 --> 01:11:46.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
other supplements

01:11:46.730 --> 01:11:50.690
can one use to try and
improve hormone profiles?

01:11:50.690 --> 01:11:52.925
And here, I realize we're
using a very broad brush

01:11:52.925 --> 01:11:54.800
because when we say
improve hormone profiles,

01:11:54.800 --> 01:11:56.220
what are we really
talking about?

01:11:56.220 --> 01:12:01.790
And for me, at least, I think
about the subjective stuff.

01:12:01.790 --> 01:12:03.650
Do people feel
like they are going

01:12:03.650 --> 01:12:05.570
to have more energy as
a consequence of doing

01:12:05.570 --> 01:12:09.140
these things, are they going to
have the more optimized libido,

01:12:09.140 --> 01:12:11.870
or are they going to have
more optimized recovery

01:12:11.870 --> 01:12:12.770
from exercise?

01:12:12.770 --> 01:12:14.510
Because, I mean,
it's not clear to me

01:12:14.510 --> 01:12:17.120
that taking one's
testosterone from 600 to 800

01:12:17.120 --> 01:12:19.700
is always going to be a good
thing, especially if estrogen

01:12:19.700 --> 01:12:21.020
is increasing in parallel.

01:12:21.020 --> 01:12:22.377
That could cause issues.

01:12:22.377 --> 01:12:23.960
It could certainly
make things better.

01:12:23.960 --> 01:12:26.120
It could certainly
make things worse.

01:12:26.120 --> 01:12:30.703
So with that backdrop, what
are some of the other things

01:12:30.703 --> 01:12:31.370
people can take?

01:12:31.370 --> 01:12:32.870
And then we'll go
back to this issue

01:12:32.870 --> 01:12:34.340
of what really is optimization.

01:12:34.340 --> 01:12:36.257
KYLE GILLETT: Let's
briefly mention vitamin D,

01:12:36.257 --> 01:12:37.440
which is also a hormone.

01:12:37.440 --> 01:12:39.140
It's actually a sterile hormone.

01:12:39.140 --> 01:12:42.980
And if you have deficient
vitamin D and you replace it,

01:12:42.980 --> 01:12:45.980
then you will optimize
your testosterone.

01:12:45.980 --> 01:12:47.240
Let's also mention boron.

01:12:47.240 --> 01:12:49.550
So if you have a
very high SHBG, boron

01:12:49.550 --> 01:12:52.040
can acutely help lower
it usually in a dose

01:12:52.040 --> 01:12:54.570
of 5 to 12 milligrams per day.

01:12:54.570 --> 01:12:56.450
It's not really a
sustained effect,

01:12:56.450 --> 01:13:00.260
but boron is depleted in
soils in many countries.

01:13:00.260 --> 01:13:03.080
I believe it's very high in
soils in Greece and Turkey.

01:13:03.080 --> 01:13:05.660
So eating dates or raisins
that are from those areas

01:13:05.660 --> 01:13:07.310
potentially have more boron.

01:13:07.310 --> 01:13:09.770
Boron also might be
one of the reasons

01:13:09.770 --> 01:13:12.530
why the reference
range for testosterone

01:13:12.530 --> 01:13:15.688
is much higher in those
countries than other countries.

01:13:15.688 --> 01:13:17.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
just to remind people,

01:13:17.480 --> 01:13:19.820
that SHBG sex hormone
binding globulin

01:13:19.820 --> 01:13:22.010
is attaching to the
testosterone molecule

01:13:22.010 --> 01:13:24.350
and limiting the amount of
so-called free testosterone

01:13:24.350 --> 01:13:26.540
that's available to have
its impact on cells.

01:13:26.540 --> 01:13:28.958
When Dr. Peter Attia
was on this podcast--

01:13:28.958 --> 01:13:30.500
in fact, sitting in
that very chair--

01:13:30.500 --> 01:13:34.310
he said that the ideal level
of free testosterone in males

01:13:34.310 --> 01:13:37.775
should be about 2% of
one's total testosterone.

01:13:37.775 --> 01:13:39.650
Would you agree with
that number or disagree?

01:13:39.650 --> 01:13:42.065
I'm sure Peter would be
fine if you said either.

01:13:42.065 --> 01:13:42.802
[CHUCKLES]

01:13:42.802 --> 01:13:44.510
KYLE GILLETT: 2% is
a good rule of thumb.

01:13:44.510 --> 01:13:48.260
Usually, the reference range
is between about 1% and 4%.

01:13:48.260 --> 01:13:51.290
Some people do have
genetic polymorphisms

01:13:51.290 --> 01:13:54.920
in SHBG, a specific
gene mutation where

01:13:54.920 --> 01:13:57.290
they have very low SHBGs.

01:13:57.290 --> 01:14:00.320
Also men that have varicose
veins in their testes,

01:14:00.320 --> 01:14:04.490
also known as varicoceles,
tend to have very high SHBGs,

01:14:04.490 --> 01:14:07.800
so that percentage would
likely be less than 2%.

01:14:07.800 --> 01:14:12.740
So just because your
percentage of free T to total T

01:14:12.740 --> 01:14:17.250
is a little bit above
or below 2%, that's OK.

01:14:17.250 --> 01:14:19.610
We just need to figure
out the reason why it is.

01:14:19.610 --> 01:14:21.068
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
How would somebody

01:14:21.068 --> 01:14:23.720
know if they have varicose
veins in their testicles,

01:14:23.720 --> 01:14:26.420
especially if their
testicles are still in--

01:14:26.420 --> 01:14:27.860
attached to their body?

01:14:27.860 --> 01:14:29.990
KYLE GILLETT: Sometimes,
it's hard to tell.

01:14:29.990 --> 01:14:31.490
There are several grades.

01:14:31.490 --> 01:14:35.490
If you have a grade 3
or a grade 4 varicocele,

01:14:35.490 --> 01:14:37.850
it has what's called a
bag of worms appearance.

01:14:37.850 --> 01:14:40.370
So think about if you've
just resistance trained,

01:14:40.370 --> 01:14:42.380
or it's a really
hot day, or you're

01:14:42.380 --> 01:14:44.600
wearing very tight
fitting clothing,

01:14:44.600 --> 01:14:46.910
then if you feel it and
almost feels like there's

01:14:46.910 --> 01:14:49.440
worms in the scrotum.

01:14:49.440 --> 01:14:50.540
The other way is to do--

01:14:50.540 --> 01:14:52.165
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's a scary visual.

01:14:52.165 --> 01:14:53.900
KYLE GILLETT:
--yeah, bag of worms.

01:14:53.900 --> 01:14:55.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, it
was just that-- yeah, anyway.

01:14:55.520 --> 01:14:57.110
I think parasites
when I hear that,

01:14:57.110 --> 01:14:58.777
but that's not what
you're referring to.

01:14:58.777 --> 01:15:01.195
You're talking about
just the texture.

01:15:01.195 --> 01:15:03.320
KYLE GILLETT: The best way
for most people to check

01:15:03.320 --> 01:15:06.950
is to valsalva for a
long period of time.

01:15:06.950 --> 01:15:10.562
When you valsalva, venous
return will decrease.

01:15:10.562 --> 01:15:12.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Can you
explain valsalva for people?

01:15:12.770 --> 01:15:15.270
KYLE GILLETT: It's bearing down
like you're lifting a weight

01:15:15.270 --> 01:15:18.920
or having a bowel movement,
where you swallow.

01:15:18.920 --> 01:15:20.780
And a lot of times,
you can almost

01:15:20.780 --> 01:15:26.460
see build up of blood in
you're jugular veins as well.

01:15:26.460 --> 01:15:29.690
So you have decreased
blood return

01:15:29.690 --> 01:15:34.692
to the heart and increased
blood in the veins themselves.

